          "id": "protection-and-security",
          "title": "Protection and Security",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 1 Concept 18",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Protection and Security: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Protection and Security\n\nProtection controls how processes and users access system resources.\n\nSecurity protects the system from unauthorized access and attacks.\n\n```text\nUser\n │\n ▼\nAuthentication\n │\n ▼\nAuthorization\n │\n ▼\nResource Access\n │\n ▼\n┌───────────────────┐\n│ File / Memory /   │\n│ CPU / Devices     │\n└───────────────────┘\n```\n\nAuthentication:\n\nDetermines who the user is.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nUsername + Password\n        │\n        ▼\n   Authentication\n```\n\nAuthorization:\n\nDetermines what the authenticated user is allowed to access.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Protection** is an internal mechanism of an operating system that controls how processes and users access system resources. It defines and enforces the rules by which access to resources such as files, memory, CPU, and devices is granted or denied. Protection operates on the principle that every access to a resource must be validated against a set of access rights.\n\n**Security** is the broader discipline of protecting the system from unauthorized access and attacks. While protection deals with internal access control among processes and users, security extends to defending the system against external threats, malicious actors, and unauthorized entry attempts.\n\nTogether, protection and security form a layered defense: protection governs *internal* resource access, while security governs *external* and adversarial access.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe system follows a sequential flow from user identification to resource access:\n\n1. **User** initiates interaction with the system.\n2. **Authentication** — Determines *who* the user is. The user presents credentials (e.g., username + password), and the system verifies them.\n3. **Authorization** — Determines *what* the authenticated user is allowed to access. Once identity is confirmed, the system checks the user's permitted rights.\n4. **Resource Access** — The user is granted access to resources only if authorization succeeds.\n5. **Resources** — Files, Memory, CPU, and Devices are the protected entities.\n\n```text\nUser\n │\n ▼\nAuthentication   ← \"Who are you?\"\n │\n ▼\nAuthorization    ← \"What may you access?\"\n │\n ▼\nResource Access\n │\n ▼\n┌───────────────────┐\n│ File / Memory /   │\n│ CPU / Devices     │\n└───────────────────┘\n```\n\nThe key principle is that **authentication must precede authorization** — a user cannot be granted rights before their identity is established.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Authentication via Username + Password\n\n```text\nUsername + Password\n        │\n        ▼\n   Authentication\n```\n\nThe user supplies a username and password. The system compares these against stored credentials. If they match, the user is authenticated; otherwise, access is denied.\n\n### Example 2: Full Flow from User to Resource\n\n```text\nUser\n │\n ▼\nAuthentication\n │\n ▼\nAuthorization\n │\n ▼\nResource Access\n │\n ▼\n┌───────────────────┐\n│ File / Memory /   │\n│ CPU / Devices     │\n└───────────────────┘\n```\n\nA user logs in (authentication), the system checks which files, memory regions, CPU time, or devices the user may use (authorization), and only then is resource access permitted.\n\n### Example 3: Distinguishing the Two Concepts\n\n- **Protection scenario:** A process attempts to write to a memory region belonging to another process. Protection mechanisms deny this access.\n- **Security scenario:** An unauthorized external party attempts to gain entry to the system. Security mechanisms defend against this attack.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nAccess control can be modeled as a relation between subjects and objects.\n\nLet:\n\n- \\( S \\) = set of subjects (users, processes)\n- \\( O \\) = set of objects (files, memory, CPU, devices)\n- \\( A \\) = set of access rights (read, write, execute, etc.)\n\nAn **access matrix** \\( M \\) is defined as:\n\n\\[\nM : S \\times O \\rightarrow 2^{A}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( M(s, o) \\subseteq A \\) is the set of rights subject \\( s \\) holds over object \\( o \\).\n\nAccess is granted if and only if:\n\n\\[\n\\text{access}(s, o, a) = \\text{true} \\iff a \\in M(s, o)\n\\]\n\nThe overall flow can be expressed as a composition:\n\n\\[\n\\text{User} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Authentication}} \\text{Identity} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Authorization}} \\text{Rights} \\xrightarrow{} \\text{Resource Access}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Protection | Security |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Scope** | Internal access control | Defense against unauthorized access and attacks |\n| **Focus** | How processes and users access system resources | Protecting the system from attacks |\n| **Question answered** | \"What may you access?\" | \"Is the system safe from threats?\" |\n| **Relation to flow** | Governs Authorization and Resource Access | Encompasses the entire flow including Authentication |\n| **Protected entities** | File / Memory / CPU / Devices | The system as a whole |\n| **Nature** | Mechanism (rules and enforcement) | Discipline (defense and safeguarding) |\n\n| Stage | Question | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Authentication** | Who is the user? | Establish identity |\n| **Authorization** | What is the user allowed to access? | Grant or deny rights |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Confusing authentication with authorization.**\n   - Authentication determines *who* the user is.\n   - Authorization determines *what* the authenticated user is allowed to access.\n   - These are distinct steps; authentication must come first.\n\n2. **Treating protection and security as identical.**\n   - Protection controls how processes and users access system resources.\n   - Security protects the system from unauthorized access and attacks.\n   - Protection is a subset/mechanism; security is the broader goal.\n\n3. **Skipping authentication before authorization.**\n   - Authorization requires a known identity. Without authentication, there is no basis for granting rights.\n\n4. **Assuming resource access is automatic after authentication.**\n   - Authentication alone does not grant access. Authorization must also succeed before resource access is permitted.\n\n5. **Overlooking the full set of protected resources.**\n   - Resources include not only Files but also Memory, CPU, and Devices.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n1. **Memorize the flow diagram.** The sequence User → Authentication → Authorization → Resource Access → (File / Memory / CPU / Devices) is a high-yield answer structure.\n\n2. **Define both terms precisely.**\n   - Protection: controls how processes and users access system resources.\n   - Security: protects the system from unauthorized access and attacks.\n\n3. **State the distinction clearly.** Authentication = *who*; Authorization = *what*. This one-line contrast is frequently tested.\n\n4. **Use the username + password example** to illustrate authentication.\n\n5. **List all four protected resource types:** File, Memory, CPU, Devices.\n\n6. **Remember the ordering rule:** Authentication precedes Authorization, which precedes Resource Access.\n\n7. **Draw the diagram** in your answer — it demonstrates the complete flow and earns structural marks.",
