          "id": "process-scheduling-example",
          "title": "Process Scheduling Example",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 1 Concept 44",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Process Scheduling Example: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Process Scheduling Example\n\nAlthough detailed scheduling algorithms are covered in Unit 2, the basic concept can be understood with a simple CPU timeline.\n\nSuppose three processes are ready:\n\n```text\nP1 = 5 ms\nP2 = 3 ms\nP3 = 2 ms\n```\n\nIf they execute sequentially in the order P1 → P2 → P3:\n\n```text\nTime →\n0        5        8       10\n│--------│--------│--------│\n   P1        P2       P3\n   5ms       3ms      2ms\n```\n\nCompletion times:\n\n```text\nP1 = 5 ms\nP2 = 8 ms\nP3 = 10 ms\n```\n\nTurnaround time, assuming all arrive at time 0:\n\n```text\nTurnaround Time = Completion Time − Arrival Time\n```\n\nTherefore:\n\n```text\nP1 = 5 − 0  = 5 ms\nP2 = 8 − 0  = 8 ms\nP3 = 10 − 0 = 10 ms\n```\n\nAverage turnaround time:\n\n```text\nAverage TAT = (5 + 8 + 10) / 3\n            = 23 / 3\n            = 7.67 ms\n```\n\nThis demonstrates the basic numerical concepts used in CPU scheduling.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Process scheduling** is the activity performed by the operating system's **scheduler** to decide which process in the ready queue is allocated the CPU and for how long. The **CPU timeline** shown above is a graphical representation of the order in which processes occupy the CPU over time.\n\nIn this example, the scheduling policy is effectively **First-Come, First-Served (FCFS)** — processes are executed sequentially in the order P1 → P2 → P3, with no preemption and no idle gaps.\n\nKey terms used in this example:\n\n- **Arrival Time (AT):** The time at which a process enters the ready queue. Here, all processes arrive at time 0.\n- **Burst Time (BT):** The CPU time required by a process. Here, P1 = 5 ms, P2 = 3 ms, P3 = 2 ms.\n- **Completion Time (CT):** The time at which a process finishes execution.\n- **Turnaround Time (TAT):** The total time from arrival to completion.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe scheduling example follows a simple sequential execution model:\n\n1. **All processes are ready at time 0.** No process has to wait for arrival, so the ready queue is fully populated at the start.\n2. **The scheduler picks the first process (P1).** P1 runs to completion, occupying the CPU from time 0 to time 5.\n3. **The scheduler picks the next process (P2).** P2 runs from time 5 to time 8.\n4. **The scheduler picks the last process (P3).** P3 runs from time 8 to time 10.\n5. **The CPU is never idle** because there is always a ready process waiting.\n\nThe CPU timeline visually confirms that the CPU is continuously busy from time 0 to time 10, and each process's completion time equals the cumulative sum of burst times up to and including that process.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Sequential Execution (as given)\n\n```text\nOrder: P1 → P2 → P3\nP1: 0 → 5\nP2: 5 → 8\nP3: 8 → 10\n```\n\nCompletion times: P1 = 5, P2 = 8, P3 = 10.\n\n### Example 2: Reordered Execution (P3 → P2 → P1)\n\nIf the same processes were executed in the order P3 → P2 → P1:\n\n```text\nTime →\n0        2        5       10\n│--------│--------│--------│\n   P3        P2       P1\n   2ms       3ms      5ms\n```\n\nCompletion times:\n\n```text\nP3 = 2 ms\nP2 = 5 ms\nP1 = 10 ms\n```\n\nTurnaround times (all arrive at 0):\n\n```text\nP3 = 2 − 0  = 2 ms\nP2 = 5 − 0  = 5 ms\nP1 = 10 − 0 = 10 ms\n```\n\nAverage TAT:\n\n```text\nAverage TAT = (2 + 5 + 10) / 3\n            = 17 / 3\n            = 5.67 ms\n```\n\nThis shows that **changing the order changes the average turnaround time**, even though the total CPU work (10 ms) is unchanged. This is the core motivation for studying scheduling algorithms.\n\n### Example 3: Comparison of Orders\n\n| Order | P1 CT | P2 CT | P3 CT | Average TAT |\n|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------------|\n| P1 → P2 → P3 | 5 | 8 | 10 | 7.67 ms |\n| P3 → P2 → P1 | 10 | 5 | 2 | 5.67 ms |\n\nThe second order gives a lower average turnaround time because shorter jobs run first.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe following formulas are used throughout this example:\n\n**Completion Time (CT):**\n\n$$\nCT_i = \\sum_{k=1}^{i} BT_k \\quad \\text{(when all arrive at time 0 and run sequentially)}\n$$\n\n**Turnaround Time (TAT):**\n\n$$\nTAT_i = CT_i - AT_i\n$$\n\n**Average Turnaround Time:**\n\n$$\n\\text{Average TAT} = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} TAT_i\n$$\n\nFor the given example (n = 3, all AT = 0):\n\n$$\n\\text{Average TAT} = \\frac{5 + 8 + 10}{3} = \\frac{23}{3} = 7.67 \\text{ ms}\n$$\n\n**Total CPU Work:**\n\n$$\n\\text{Total Work} = BT_1 + BT_2 + BT_3 = 5 + 3 + 2 = 10 \\text{ ms}\n$$\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | P1 → P2 → P3 | P3 → P2 → P1 |\n|--------|--------------|--------------|\n| First process CT | 5 ms | 2 ms |\n| Second process CT | 8 ms | 5 ms |\n| Third process CT | 10 ms | 10 ms |\n| Average TAT | 7.67 ms | 5.67 ms |\n| Total CPU work | 10 ms | 10 ms |\n| CPU idle time | 0 ms | 0 ms |\n\n**Observation:** Total CPU work and CPU idle time are identical in both orders, but average turnaround time differs. This illustrates that **scheduling order affects performance metrics** even when the workload is fixed.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing completion time with turnaround time.** Completion time is measured from time 0; turnaround time is measured from each process's arrival time. When all arrive at 0, they are numerically equal — but this is a special case, not a general rule.\n- **Forgetting to subtract arrival time.** If arrival times were non-zero, using completion time directly as turnaround time would be incorrect.\n- **Assuming order does not matter.** As shown in Example 2, reordering the same processes changes the average turnaround time.\n- **Adding burst times incorrectly.** Completion time of a process is the cumulative sum of burst times of all processes up to and including it, not just its own burst time.\n- **Rounding too early.** In the average TAT calculation, 23/3 should be kept as a fraction or rounded only at the end (7.67 ms).\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Always draw the CPU timeline.** A clear timeline makes completion times obvious and reduces arithmetic errors.\n- **State the formula before substituting values.** Writing `TAT = CT − AT` first shows the examiner you know the definition.\n- **Check that total CPU work equals the final completion time** when there is no idle time. Here, 5 + 3 + 2 = 10 = CT of the last process. This is a useful sanity check.\n- **Mention the assumption that all processes arrive at time 0.** This assumption is what makes `TAT = CT` in this example.\n- **Show the average as a fraction before decimal.** `23/3 = 7.67 ms` demonstrates precision and avoids rounding disputes.\n- **Use this example as a foundation** for Unit 2 algorithms (FCFS, SJF, etc.) — the same timeline and TAT formulas apply, only the ordering rule changes.",
