          "id": "numerical-formula-sheet-for-unit-1",
          "title": "Numerical Formula Sheet for Unit 1",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 1 Concept 47",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Numerical Formula Sheet for Unit 1: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Numerical Formula Sheet for Unit 1\n\n### Academic Definition\n\nThe **Numerical Formula Sheet for Unit 1** consolidates the fundamental quantitative relationships used in **CPU scheduling** analysis. These formulas provide a standardized mathematical framework for evaluating the performance of scheduling algorithms such as FCFS, SJF, SRTF, Round Robin, and Priority Scheduling. Each metric captures a distinct dimension of scheduler efficiency — from individual process experience (Turnaround Time, Waiting Time, Response Time) to system-wide performance (CPU Utilization, Throughput). Together, they form the basis for comparing scheduling policies in Operating Systems.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nEvery process in a CPU scheduling problem is characterized by two given quantities: its **Arrival Time (AT)** and its **CPU Burst Time (BT)**. From the schedule (typically visualized using a **Gantt Chart**), we derive the **Completion Time (CT)** and the **First CPU Start Time**. All other metrics are then computed as derived quantities:\n\n1. Determine the schedule order and compute **Completion Time** for each process from the Gantt chart.\n2. Compute **Turnaround Time** as the total time from arrival to completion.\n3. Compute **Waiting Time** by subtracting the actual CPU service time from TAT.\n4. Compute **Response Time** using the first time the process gets the CPU.\n5. Aggregate using averages, and evaluate system-level metrics (Utilization, Throughput).\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\nConsider three processes with the following Gantt chart:\n\n```text\nTime →\n0       4       7       10\n│-------│-------│--------│\n   P1      P2       P3\n```\n\nAssume: P1 arrives at 0, P2 at 1, P3 at 2.\n\n| Process | AT | BT | CT | TAT = CT − AT | WT = TAT − BT | RT |\n|---------|----|----|----|---------------|---------------|----|\n| P1      | 0  | 4  | 4  | 4             | 0             | 0  |\n| P2      | 1  | 3  | 7  | 6             | 3             | 3  |\n| P3      | 2  | 3  | 10 | 8             | 5             | 5  |\n\n- **Average TAT** = (4 + 6 + 8) / 3 = 6\n- **Average WT** = (0 + 3 + 5) / 3 ≈ 2.67\n- **Average RT** = (0 + 3 + 5) / 3 ≈ 2.67\n- **CPU Utilization** = (10 / 10) × 100 = 100%\n- **Throughput** = 3 / 10 = 0.3 processes per unit time\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\n$$\n\\text{TAT} = \\text{CT} - \\text{AT}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{WT} = \\text{TAT} - \\text{BT}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{RT} = \\text{First CPU Start Time} - \\text{AT}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Average TAT} = \\frac{\\sum \\text{TAT}_i}{n}, \\quad\n\\text{Average WT} = \\frac{\\sum \\text{WT}_i}{n}, \\quad\n\\text{Average RT} = \\frac{\\sum \\text{RT}_i}{n}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{CPU Utilization} = \\left(\\frac{\\text{CPU Busy Time}}{\\text{Total Time}}\\right) \\times 100\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Throughput} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Processes Completed}}{\\text{Total Time}}\n$$\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Metric | Depends On | Lower/Higher is Better | Typical Use |\n|--------|-----------|------------------------|-------------|\n| Turnaround Time | CT, AT | Lower is better | Overall process latency |\n| Waiting Time | TAT, BT | Lower is better | Time wasted in ready queue |\n| Response Time | First CPU Start, AT | Lower is better | Interactive system responsiveness |\n| CPU Utilization | Busy / Total Time | Higher is better | System efficiency |\n| Throughput | Completed / Total Time | Higher is better | Work completed per unit time |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- Confusing **Turnaround Time** with **Waiting Time** — TAT includes the burst time; WT does not.\n- Using **Arrival Time** instead of **First CPU Start Time** when computing Response Time.\n- Forgetting that **Response Time = Waiting Time** in non-preemptive scheduling (since the process starts immediately once scheduled).\n- Mixing up **Completion Time** with **Burst Time** in the TAT formula.\n- Computing **CPU Utilization** without accounting for idle gaps in the Gantt chart.\n- Dividing by the wrong count when computing averages (must divide by number of processes, not total time).\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Always draw the **Gantt chart** first — it makes CT extraction trivial.\n- Build a table with columns: **Process, AT, BT, CT, TAT, WT, RT** — this avoids arithmetic slips.\n- Remember the identity: **TAT = WT + BT**, useful for cross-checking.\n- For **Round Robin**, Response Time is usually much smaller than Waiting Time — highlight this in comparisons.\n- Verify **CPU Utilization ≤ 100%**; if it exceeds, you've likely double-counted busy time.\n- For **Throughput**, keep units consistent (processes per unit time).\n- Show formulas explicitly in answers — examiners award step marks.",
