          "id": "shortest-remaining-time-first-srtf",
          "title": "Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 2 Concept 6",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF): definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)** is a **preemptive CPU scheduling algorithm** that is the preemptive version of **Shortest Job First (SJF)**. At any given point in time, the scheduler selects the process whose **remaining CPU burst time** is the smallest among all currently available (arrived) processes. Because it is preemptive, if a **new process arrives** with a shorter remaining time than the currently running process, the **currently running process is preempted** and the CPU is given to the newly arrived shorter job.\n\nSRTF is also referred to as **Preemptive SJF** or **Shortest Remaining Time Next (SRTN)**. It is a **dynamic priority** algorithm where priority is inversely proportional to the remaining burst time — the smaller the remaining time, the higher the priority.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe core idea of SRTF is to always keep the CPU busy with the job that will finish the soonest, minimizing the average waiting time. The algorithm operates as follows:\n\n```text\nSRTF\n              │\n              ▼\n     Compare remaining times\n              │\n              ▼\n     Shortest remaining job\n              │\n              ▼\n             CPU\n```\n\n1. **At every arrival or completion event**, the scheduler compares the **remaining burst times** of all ready processes.\n2. The process with the **smallest remaining time** is dispatched to the CPU.\n3. If a **new process arrives** with a shorter remaining time than the currently running process, the current process is **preempted** and placed back in the ready queue.\n4. This comparison is repeated at every scheduling point (arrival, preemption, or completion).\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n#### Numerical Example (from notes)\n\n| Process | Arrival Time | Burst Time |\n| ------- | ------------ | ---------- |\n| P1 | 0 | 8 |\n| P2 | 1 | 4 |\n| P3 | 2 | 2 |\n\n**At time 0:**\n\nP1 starts (only process available).\n\n**At time 1:**\n\n```text\nP1 remaining = 7\nP2 burst     = 4\n```\n\nP2 is shorter → **P1 is preempted**.\n\n**At time 2:**\n\n```text\nP2 remaining = 3\nP3 burst     = 2\n```\n\nP3 is shorter → **P2 is preempted**.\n\n**Execution timeline:**\n\n```text\n0    1    2    4       7           14\n│ P1 │ P2 │ P3 │  P2   │     P1    │\n```\n\n**Completion times:**\n\n```text\nP3 = 4\nP2 = 7\nP1 = 14\n```\n\n**Turnaround time (TAT = Completion − Arrival):**\n\n```text\nP1 = 14 − 0 = 14\nP2 = 7 − 1 = 6\nP3 = 4 − 2 = 2\n```\n\n**Waiting time (WT = TAT − Burst):**\n\n```text\nP1 = 14 − 8 = 6\nP2 = 6 − 4 = 2\nP3 = 2 − 2 = 0\n```\n\n**Average waiting time:**\n\n```text\nAWT = (6 + 2 + 0) / 3\n    = 2.67 ms\n```\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe key performance metrics for SRTF are computed using the following standard formulas:\n\n$$\n\\text{Turnaround Time (TAT)} = \\text{Completion Time} - \\text{Arrival Time}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Waiting Time (WT)} = \\text{Turnaround Time} - \\text{Burst Time}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Average Waiting Time (AWT)} = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} WT_i}{n}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Average Turnaround Time (ATAT)} = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} TAT_i}{n}\n$$\n\nFor the example above:\n\n$$\n\\text{AWT} = \\frac{6 + 2 + 0}{3} = 2.67 \\text{ ms}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{ATAT} = \\frac{14 + 6 + 2}{3} = \\frac{22}{3} = 7.33 \\text{ ms}\n$$\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Feature | SRTF (Preemptive SJF) | SJF (Non-Preemptive) |\n| ------- | --------------------- | -------------------- |\n| **Preemption** | Yes — running process can be preempted | No — once started, runs to completion |\n| **Selection Criterion** | Shortest **remaining** time | Shortest **total burst** time |\n| **Response to new arrivals** | May preempt current process | New process waits until current finishes |\n| **Average Waiting Time** | Generally lower (optimal) | Higher than SRTF |\n| **Overhead** | Higher (frequent context switches) | Lower (no preemption overhead) |\n| **Starvation** | Possible for long processes | Possible for long processes |\n| **Complexity** | Requires tracking remaining times | Requires knowing burst times upfront |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Confusing SRTF with SJF** — SRTF is **preemptive**; SJF is **non-preemptive**. The key difference is whether a running process can be interrupted by a newly arriving shorter job.\n2. **Forgetting to recompute remaining time** — At each arrival, the remaining time of the running process must be updated before comparison.\n3. **Incorrect waiting time calculation** — Waiting time is **not** simply the gap before first execution; it includes all time spent in the ready queue across preemptions.\n4. **Assuming SRTF always preempts** — Preemption occurs **only** if the new process has a **strictly shorter** remaining time.\n5. **Miscalculating completion time** — Completion time is the moment the process **finishes its last burst segment**, not when it first gets the CPU.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- **Always draw the Gantt chart** — it is the fastest way to visualize preemptions and avoid errors in completion times.\n- **Track remaining times in a table** at each time unit or event to stay organized.\n- **Remember the formula chain:** Completion → Turnaround → Waiting. Errors propagate if completion times are wrong.\n- **SRTF gives the minimum average waiting time** among all scheduling algorithms — a commonly asked theoretical point.\n- **Mention starvation** as a drawback — long processes may wait indefinitely if short processes keep arriving.\n- **Know the overhead trade-off** — SRTF has more context switches than SJF, which is a practical disadvantage.\n- **Burst times must be known in advance** — this is a key limitation, as real systems cannot predict burst times accurately.",
