          "id": "real-time-scheduling",
          "title": "Real-Time Scheduling",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 2 Concept 10",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Real-Time Scheduling: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Real-Time Scheduling\n\nReal-time scheduling is used when processes have deadlines.\n\n```text\nTask\n         │\n         ▼\n    Release Time\n         │\n         ▼\n      Execution\n         │\n         ▼\n      Deadline\n```\n\nA real-time process can be represented as:\n\n```text\nRelease Time ───────────────► Deadline\n     │                           │\n     ▼                           ▼\n     ├──────── Execution ────────┤\n```\n\nTwo major approaches are:\n\n**Rate Monotonic Scheduling (RMS)**\n\nA fixed-priority algorithm.\n\nTasks with shorter periods receive higher priority.\n\n```text\nShorter Period\n      │\n      ▼\nHigher Priority\n```\n\n**Earliest Deadline First (EDF)**\n\nThe process with the earliest deadline gets the CPU.\n\n```text\nP1 deadline = 20\nP2 deadline = 12\nP3 deadline = 18\n\nExecution priority:\n\nP2 → P3 → P1\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Real-Time Scheduling** is the discipline of assigning CPU time to processes (tasks) such that each task completes its execution within a specified **deadline**. Unlike general-purpose scheduling, correctness in real-time systems depends not only on the logical result of computation but also on the **time at which the result is produced**.\n\nA real-time task is characterized by:\n- **Release Time** — the instant at which the task becomes ready for execution.\n- **Execution Time** — the CPU time required to complete the task.\n- **Deadline** — the instant by which the task must complete.\n\nReal-time scheduling is broadly classified into:\n- **Fixed-priority algorithms** — priorities are assigned once and do not change (e.g., RMS).\n- **Dynamic-priority algorithms** — priorities may change at runtime (e.g., EDF).\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe core principle of real-time scheduling is to **meet deadlines** by ordering task execution according to urgency.\n\n### General Flow\n\n```text\nTask arrives (Release Time)\n        │\n        ▼\nScheduler examines deadlines / periods\n        │\n        ▼\nHighest-urgency task selected\n        │\n        ▼\nExecution proceeds\n        │\n        ▼\nTask completes before Deadline\n```\n\n### RMS Working Principle\n\n- Priorities are **fixed** at design time.\n- The task with the **shortest period** is given the **highest priority**.\n- At every scheduling point, the highest-priority ready task runs.\n\n```text\nShorter Period\n      │\n      ▼\nHigher Priority\n```\n\n### EDF Working Principle\n\n- Priorities are **dynamic**.\n- At each scheduling instant, the task with the **earliest deadline** is selected.\n- Priority ordering can change as deadlines approach.\n\n```text\nP1 deadline = 20\nP2 deadline = 12\nP3 deadline = 18\n\nExecution priority:\n\nP2 → P3 → P1\n```\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — EDF Ordering\n\nGiven three tasks with deadlines:\n\n| Task | Deadline |\n|------|----------|\n| P1   | 20       |\n| P2   | 12       |\n| P3   | 18       |\n\nEDF selects the task with the earliest deadline first:\n\n```text\nExecution priority:\n\nP2 → P3 → P1\n```\n\nBecause **12 < 18 < 20**, the order is P2, then P3, then P1.\n\n### Example 2 — RMS Priority Assignment\n\nUnder RMS, priority is derived from the period:\n\n```text\nShorter Period\n      │\n      ▼\nHigher Priority\n```\n\nThus, a task with a shorter period is scheduled ahead of a task with a longer period, regardless of arrival order.\n\n### Example 3 — Timeline Representation\n\n```text\nRelease Time ───────────────► Deadline\n     │                           │\n     ▼                           ▼\n     ├──────── Execution ────────┤\n```\n\nThe execution window lies between the release time and the deadline. The scheduler must fit the execution within this window.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\n### Task Model\n\nA real-time task can be represented as a tuple:\n\n$$\nT_i = (r_i,\\ e_i,\\ d_i)\n$$\n\nwhere:\n- $r_i$ = release time\n- $e_i$ = execution time\n- $d_i$ = deadline\n\n### Timeline Constraint\n\nFor a task to be schedulable:\n\n$$\nr_i + e_i \\leq d_i\n$$\n\nThat is, the release time plus execution time must not exceed the deadline.\n\n### RMS Priority Rule\n\nFor two tasks $T_i$ and $T_j$ with periods $p_i$ and $p_j$:\n\n$$\np_i < p_j \\implies \\text{priority}(T_i) > \\text{priority}(T_j)\n$$\n\n### EDF Priority Rule\n\nAt any scheduling instant, the selected task is:\n\n$$\nT_{\\text{selected}} = \\arg\\min_i (d_i)\n$$\n\nThe task with the smallest deadline is chosen.\n\n### EDF Ordering Example\n\nGiven deadlines $d_1 = 20$, $d_2 = 12$, $d_3 = 18$:\n\n$$\n\\min(20, 12, 18) = 12 \\Rightarrow P2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\min(20, 18) = 18 \\Rightarrow P3\n$$\n\n$$\n\\min(20) = 20 \\Rightarrow P1\n$$\n\nResulting order:\n\n$$\nP2 \\rightarrow P3 \\rightarrow P1\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Feature | Rate Monotonic Scheduling (RMS) | Earliest Deadline First (EDF) |\n|---------|--------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| Priority type | Fixed | Dynamic |\n| Priority basis | Shorter period → higher priority | Earliest deadline → highest priority |\n| Priority changes | No | Yes, at runtime |\n| Selection rule | Highest priority ready task | Task with earliest deadline |\n| Example ordering | Based on period length | P2 → P3 → P1 (for deadlines 12, 18, 20) |\n| Representation | `Shorter Period → Higher Priority` | `Earliest Deadline → CPU` |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Confusing period with deadline** — In RMS, priority is based on the **period**, not the deadline.\n2. **Assuming RMS priorities change** — RMS is a **fixed-priority** algorithm; priorities are assigned once.\n3. **Assuming EDF priorities are static** — EDF is **dynamic**; the earliest-deadline task can change over time.\n4. **Ignoring the release time** — Execution cannot begin before the release time.\n5. **Misordering EDF tasks** — Always pick the **smallest** deadline value first (e.g., 12 before 18 before 20).\n6. **Forgetting the deadline constraint** — A task is only correct if it finishes by its deadline: $r_i + e_i \\leq d_i$.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Remember the RMS rule:** *Shorter Period → Higher Priority.*\n- **Remember the EDF rule:** *Earliest Deadline → CPU.*\n- **Draw the timeline:** Use the `Release Time → Execution → Deadline` diagram to visualize constraints.\n- **For EDF ordering questions:** Sort tasks by deadline in ascending order.\n  - Deadlines 20, 12, 18 → order **P2 → P3 → P1**.\n- **Distinguish fixed vs. dynamic:** RMS = fixed-priority; EDF = dynamic-priority.\n- **State the task tuple:** $T_i = (r_i, e_i, d_i)$ and the schedulability condition $r_i + e_i \\leq d_i$.\n- **Use the comparison table** to quickly answer \"difference between RMS and EDF\" questions.\n- **Avoid mixing up period and deadline** — this is the most common exam trap.",
