          "notes": "## Deadlock\n\nA deadlock is a situation in which a set of processes is permanently blocked because every process is waiting for a resource held by another process in the same set.\n\n```text\n             ┌──────────────┐\n             │   Process P1  │\n             └──────┬───────┘\n                    │ holds R1\n                    ▼\n             ┌──────────────┐\n             │   Resource R1 │\n             └──────┬───────┘\n                    │\n                    │ requested by P2\n                    ▼\n             ┌──────────────┐\n             │   Process P2  │\n             └──────┬───────┘\n                    │ holds R2\n                    ▼\n             ┌──────────────┐\n             │   Resource R2 │\n             └──────┬───────┘\n                    │\n                    │ requested by P1\n                    └──────────────► P1\n```\n\nTherefore:\n\n- P1 waits for R2\n- R2 is held by P2\n- P2 waits for R1\n- R1 is held by P1\n\nNeither process can continue.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nA **deadlock** is a state in an operating system in which a **set of processes** is **permanently blocked** because **every process** in that set is **waiting for a resource** that is **held by another process** within the **same set**. Because each process is waiting on a resource that will never be released by its current holder (which is itself blocked), no process in the set can ever make progress. This is a **permanent** condition — it does not resolve on its own without external intervention.\n\nFormally, a deadlock exists if there is a set of waiting processes $\\{P_1, P_2, \\dots, P_n\\}$ such that:\n\n- $P_1$ is waiting for a resource held by $P_2$\n- $P_2$ is waiting for a resource held by $P_3$\n- $\\dots$\n- $P_n$ is waiting for a resource held by $P_1$\n\nThis forms a **circular wait** among the processes.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe deadlock in the given example arises through the following sequence of events:\n\n1. **Process P1 acquires Resource R1.** P1 now *holds* R1.\n2. **Process P2 acquires Resource R2.** P2 now *holds* R2.\n3. **P1 requests Resource R2.** Since R2 is already held by P2, P1 must **wait**.\n4. **P2 requests Resource R1.** Since R1 is already held by P1, P2 must **wait**.\n\nAt this point:\n\n- P1 waits for R2, which is held by P2.\n- P2 waits for R1, which is held by P1.\n\nThis creates a **cycle of dependencies**:\n\n$$P_1 \\rightarrow R_2 \\rightarrow P_2 \\rightarrow R_1 \\rightarrow P_1$$\n\nBecause neither P1 nor P2 can release the resource it holds until it obtains the resource it is waiting for, **neither process can continue**. The system is in a deadlock.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Two Processes, Two Resources (from the notes)\n\n- **P1 holds R1**, requests R2.\n- **P2 holds R2**, requests R1.\n\nResult: P1 waits for R2 (held by P2); P2 waits for R1 (held by P1). Neither can continue.\n\n### Example 2: Extending the Cycle\n\nThe same principle extends to more than two processes. If:\n\n- P1 holds R1 and requests R2,\n- P2 holds R2 and requests R3,\n- P3 holds R3 and requests R1,\n\nthen a circular wait exists among P1, P2, and P3, and all three are permanently blocked.\n\n### Example 3: The General Case\n\nIn general, whenever a set of processes forms a **closed chain of waiting**, where each process waits for a resource held by the next process in the chain, and the last process waits for a resource held by the first, a deadlock occurs.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nA deadlock can be represented as a **wait-for cycle**. For a set of processes $\\{P_1, P_2, \\dots, P_n\\}$ and resources $\\{R_1, R_2, \\dots, R_n\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\text{Held}(P_i) = R_i \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\text{Requested}(P_i) = R_{i+1}\n$$\n\nwith indices taken modulo $n$ (so $P_n$ requests $R_1$).\n\nThe deadlock condition is:\n\n$$\n\\forall i \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\}: \\; P_i \\text{ waits for } R_{i+1} \\text{ held by } P_{i+1}\n$$\n\nFor the two-process case in the notes:\n\n$$\n\\text{Held}(P_1) = R_1,\\quad \\text{Requested}(P_1) = R_2\n$$\n$$\n\\text{Held}(P_2) = R_2,\\quad \\text{Requested}(P_2) = R_1\n$$\n\nThis yields the cycle:\n\n$$\nP_1 \\rightarrow R_2 \\rightarrow P_2 \\rightarrow R_1 \\rightarrow P_1\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Process P1 | Process P2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Resource held | R1 | R2 |\n| Resource requested | R2 | R1 |\n| Waiting for | R2 (held by P2) | R1 (held by P1) |\n| Can continue? | No | No |\n| Reason blocked | R2 unavailable | R1 unavailable |\n\n| Aspect | Deadlock (as described) |\n|---|---|\n| Nature of block | Permanent |\n| Resolution | Does not resolve on its own |\n| Cause | Circular wait among processes |\n| Processes involved | A set of processes, each waiting on another |\n| Key condition | Every process waits for a resource held by another in the same set |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Assuming a single waiting process is a deadlock.** A deadlock requires a **set** of processes, each waiting on a resource held by another in the same set — not just one process waiting.\n- **Confusing a temporary wait with a deadlock.** A deadlock is **permanent**; a process waiting for a resource that will eventually be released is not deadlocked.\n- **Ignoring the circular dependency.** The defining feature is the **cycle**: P1 waits for R2 held by P2, and P2 waits for R1 held by P1. Without the cycle, there is no deadlock.\n- **Thinking the deadlock resolves itself.** As stated, neither process can continue — external intervention is required.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Define deadlock precisely:** \"A set of processes is permanently blocked because every process is waiting for a resource held by another process in the same set.\"\n- **Draw the cycle.** Reproduce the diagram showing P1 holds R1, P2 holds R2, P1 waits for R2, and P2 waits for R1. Clearly mark the four facts:\n  - P1 waits for R2\n  - R2 is held by P2\n  - P2 waits for R1\n  - R1 is held by P1\n- **State the conclusion explicitly:** \"Neither process can continue.\"\n- **Use the wait-for cycle notation** $P_1 \\rightarrow R_2 \\rightarrow P_2 \\rightarrow R_1 \\rightarrow P_1$ to demonstrate the circular dependency.\n- **Emphasize permanence** — the block does not resolve on its own.",
