          "id": "resource-preemption",
          "title": "Resource Preemption",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 3 Concept 20",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Resource Preemption: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Resource Preemption\n\nThe OS temporarily takes resources from one process and gives them to another.\n\n```text\nP1 ──holds──► R1\n\n        R1\n        │\n        │ preempt\n        ▼\n      OS\n        │\n        ▼\n       P2\n```\n\nIssues include:\n\n- Selecting a victim\n- Rollback\n- Starvation\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Resource Preemption** is a deadlock-recovery technique in which the operating system forcibly reclaims (takes away) a resource that is currently held by one process and reallocates it to another process. It is one of the classical approaches to **deadlock recovery**, alongside process termination. Rather than killing a process outright, the OS temporarily removes one or more of its held resources so that a blocked process can proceed, thereby breaking the circular-wait condition that sustains a deadlock.\n\nThe technique is applied only after a deadlock has been detected. The OS acts as an arbiter that intervenes in the normal \"hold-and-wait\" behavior of processes by revoking ownership of a resource from a **victim** process and transferring it to a requesting process.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\n1. **Deadlock detection** identifies that a set of processes is deadlocked.\n2. The OS chooses a **victim** — a process whose resources will be preempted.\n3. The chosen resource(s) are **taken away** from the victim and given to another process (typically one blocked in the deadlock cycle).\n4. The victim is **rolled back** to a safe state (a checkpoint) so it can later resume and re-acquire the resource.\n5. The OS must ensure the victim does not **starve** — i.e., it is not repeatedly chosen as the victim.\n\nThe core idea is that preemption breaks the resource-holding chain that keeps the deadlock cycle intact.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Preempting a Printer\n\n```text\nP1 ──holds──► Printer\nP2 ──holds──► Scanner\nP1 ──wants──► Scanner\nP2 ──wants──► Printer\n```\n\nBoth processes wait forever. The OS selects **P1** as the victim, preempts the **Printer** from P1, and gives it to P2. P2 completes, releases the Scanner, and P1 can then resume.\n\n### Example 2 — Preempting a Memory Frame\n\nA process holding a locked memory frame needed by another process has that frame preempted. The victim is rolled back to a checkpoint and re-executes the portion of code that required the frame.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nPreemption cost can be modeled as the sum of victim-selection and rollback overhead:\n\n$$\nC_{preempt} = C_{select} + C_{rollback} + C_{reacquire}\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $C_{select}$ = cost of choosing a victim\n- $C_{rollback}$ = cost of rolling the victim back to a safe state\n- $C_{reacquire}$ = cost for the victim to re-acquire the resource later\n\nVictim selection is often expressed as a weighted function of measurable factors:\n\n$$\n\\text{Victim Score} = w_1 \\cdot (\\text{resources held}) + w_2 \\cdot (\\text{time run}) + w_3 \\cdot (\\text{remaining work})\n$$\n\nThe process with the **lowest** score (fewest resources, least time, most remaining work) is typically chosen as the victim.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Resource Preemption | Process Termination |\n|---|---|---|\n| Action taken | Temporarily takes resources | Kills the process |\n| Recoverability | Process can resume after rollback | Process must restart |\n| Overhead | Rollback cost | Restart cost |\n| Starvation risk | Yes (repeated victim selection) | Yes (repeated termination) |\n| Granularity | Per-resource | Per-process |\n| Complexity | Higher (needs checkpoints) | Lower |\n\n| Victim Selection Factor | Consideration |\n|---|---|\n| Resources held | Prefer victim holding few resources |\n| Time consumed | Prefer victim that has run least |\n| Remaining work | Prefer victim with most work left |\n| Rollback feasibility | Prefer victim with a valid checkpoint |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Assuming preemption is free.** It is not — rollback and re-acquisition add overhead.\n- **Ignoring starvation.** If the same process is always chosen as victim, it may never finish.\n- **Confusing preemption with termination.** Preemption is *temporary*; termination is *permanent*.\n- **Forgetting rollback.** A preempted process cannot simply continue; it must be restored to a safe state.\n- **Preempting without a checkpoint.** Without a saved state, rollback is impossible.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Always mention the **three issues**: victim selection, rollback, and starvation.\n- Distinguish clearly between **resource preemption** and **process termination** as recovery methods.\n- Use the diagram to show the OS acting as the intermediary between the holder and the requester.\n- State that preemption is a **deadlock recovery** technique, applied *after* detection.\n- Remember the victim-selection criteria: resources held, time run, remaining work.\n- Mention that preemption breaks the **circular-wait** condition.",
