          "id": "memory-allocation-numerical",
          "title": "Memory Allocation Numerical",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 3 Concept 38",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Memory Allocation Numerical: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Memory Allocation Numerical\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Memory Allocation** is the process by which an operating system assigns portions of main memory (RAM) to processes requesting space. In the context of **contiguous memory allocation**, each process is allocated a single continuous block of memory. When free memory is scattered across multiple non-adjacent regions (called **holes** or **free holes**), the allocator must decide which hole to assign to an incoming process.\n\nThe three classical placement strategies are:\n\n- **First Fit** — allocate the first hole that is large enough.\n- **Best Fit** — allocate the smallest hole that is large enough.\n- **Worst Fit** — allocate the largest available hole.\n\nThis numerical demonstrates **First Fit** allocation applied to a fixed set of free holes and a sequence of process requests.\n\n---\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe First Fit algorithm scans the list of free holes **from the beginning** and assigns the incoming process to the **first hole whose size is greater than or equal to the process size**. If the hole is larger than the request, the leftover portion becomes a **new (smaller) hole** available for future allocations. If no hole is large enough, the process is **not allocated** (it must wait or be swapped out).\n\n**Given free holes:**\n\n```text\n100 KB\n500 KB\n200 KB\n300 KB\n600 KB\n```\n\n**Given processes:**\n\n```text\nP1 = 212 KB\nP2 = 417 KB\nP3 = 112 KB\nP4 = 426 KB\n```\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Examples (Step-by-Step First Fit Trace)\n\n#### Step 1 — Allocate P1 = 212 KB\n\nScan holes from the top:\n\n- 100 KB → too small\n- 500 KB → **fits** ✅\n\nAllocate P1 in the 500 KB hole. Remaining fragment:\n\n```text\n500 − 212 = 288 KB\n```\n\nUpdated hole list:\n\n```text\n100, 288, 200, 300, 600\n```\n\n#### Step 2 — Allocate P2 = 417 KB\n\nScan holes:\n\n- 100 → too small\n- 288 → too small\n- 200 → too small\n- 300 → too small\n- 600 → **fits** ✅\n\nAllocate P2 in the 600 KB hole. Remaining fragment:\n\n```text\n600 − 417 = 183 KB\n```\n\nUpdated hole list:\n\n```text\n100, 288, 200, 300, 183\n```\n\n#### Step 3 — Allocate P3 = 112 KB\n\nScan holes:\n\n- 100 → too small\n- 288 → **fits** ✅\n\nAllocate P3 in the 288 KB hole. Remaining fragment:\n\n```text\n288 − 112 = 176 KB\n```\n\nUpdated hole list:\n\n```text\n100, 176, 200, 300, 183\n```\n\n#### Step 4 — Allocate P4 = 426 KB\n\nScan holes:\n\n- 100 → too small\n- 176 → too small\n- 200 → too small\n- 300 → too small\n- 183 → too small\n\n**No suitable block found.** P4 is **not allocated**.\n\n---\n\n### Final Result\n\n```text\nP1 → 500 KB\nP2 → 600 KB\nP3 → 288 KB remainder\nP4 → Not allocated\n```\n\n---\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nFor each allocation, the **remaining hole size** is computed as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Remaining} = \\text{Hole Size} - \\text{Process Size}\n$$\n\nFor P1:\n\n$$\n500 - 212 = 288 \\text{ KB}\n$$\n\nFor P2:\n\n$$\n600 - 417 = 183 \\text{ KB}\n$$\n\nFor P3:\n\n$$\n288 - 112 = 176 \\text{ KB}\n$$\n\nFor P4, since no hole satisfies $\\text{Hole Size} \\geq 426$, allocation fails.\n\n**Total free memory after allocation:**\n\n$$\n100 + 176 + 200 + 300 + 183 = 959 \\text{ KB}\n$$\n\n**Total allocated memory:**\n\n$$\n212 + 417 + 112 = 741 \\text{ KB}\n$$\n\n**Total memory (holes + allocated):**\n\n$$\n959 + 741 = 1700 \\text{ KB}\n$$\n\nNote: P4 (426 KB) cannot be satisfied even though **959 KB** of total free memory exists — this is **external fragmentation**.\n\n---\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Algorithm | Rule | P1 (212) | P2 (417) | P3 (112) | P4 (426) |\n|-----------|------|----------|----------|----------|----------|\n| **First Fit** | First hole large enough | 500 | 600 | 288 | Not allocated |\n| **Best Fit** | Smallest hole large enough | 500 | 600 | 288 | Not allocated |\n| **Worst Fit** | Largest hole available | 600 | 500 | 300 | Not allocated |\n\nIn this particular numerical, all three algorithms yield the **same final outcome** for P4 (not allocated), because the total largest remaining hole after P1–P3 is still smaller than 426 KB.\n\n---\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Forgetting to update the hole list** after each allocation — the remainder must be re-inserted in the correct position.\n2. **Scanning from the wrong end** — First Fit always scans from the **beginning** of the hole list.\n3. **Confusing First Fit with Best Fit** — Best Fit would pick the *smallest* suitable hole, not the first.\n4. **Assuming P4 can be allocated** because total free memory (959 KB) exceeds 426 KB — this ignores **contiguous allocation** constraints.\n5. **Miscalculating remainders** — always subtract process size from hole size, not the other way around.\n\n---\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- **Always show the hole list after each allocation** — examiners award step marks.\n- **State the algorithm clearly** (First Fit / Best Fit / Worst Fit) before starting.\n- **Check for external fragmentation** — mention it explicitly when a process cannot be allocated despite sufficient total free memory.\n- **Draw a table** of process → hole → remainder for clarity.\n- **Verify arithmetic** — simple subtraction errors are the most common mark-losing mistake.\n- **Remember**: First Fit is generally the **fastest** to implement but may cause fragmentation at the beginning of memory.",
