          "id": "page-table",
          "title": "Page Table",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 3 Concept 41",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Page Table: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Page Table\n\nA page table maps page numbers to frame numbers.\n\nExample:\n\n| Page | Frame |\n| ---- | ----- |\n| 0 | 3 |\n| 1 | 7 |\n| 2 | 1 |\n| 3 | 5 |\n\nTherefore:\n\n```text\nPage 0 → Frame 3\nPage 1 → Frame 7\nPage 2 → Frame 1\nPage 3 → Frame 5\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nA **page table** is a data structure used by a virtual memory system in an operating system to store the mapping between **virtual page numbers** and **physical frame numbers**. Each process typically maintains its own page table, which the Memory Management Unit (MMU) consults during address translation to convert a virtual address into a physical address. The page table entry (PTE) for a given page contains the corresponding frame number, allowing the system to locate where that page currently resides in physical memory.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe page table operates as the core lookup mechanism in **paging-based virtual memory**. The process works as follows:\n\n1. A program generates a **virtual address**, which is divided into a **page number** and an **offset**.\n2. The **page number** is used as an index into the page table.\n3. The page table returns the corresponding **frame number**.\n4. The **frame number** is combined with the original **offset** to form the **physical address**.\n5. The physical address is then used to access the actual data in main memory.\n\nUsing the example above, if a program references **Page 2**, the page table lookup returns **Frame 1**, and the offset is applied within that frame to reach the exact physical location.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Basic Lookup\n\nGiven the page table:\n\n| Page | Frame |\n| ---- | ----- |\n| 0 | 3 |\n| 1 | 7 |\n| 2 | 1 |\n| 3 | 5 |\n\n- A reference to **Page 0** resolves to **Frame 3**.\n- A reference to **Page 1** resolves to **Frame 7**.\n- A reference to **Page 2** resolves to **Frame 1**.\n- A reference to **Page 3** resolves to **Frame 5**.\n\n### Example 2: Address Translation Walkthrough\n\nSuppose a virtual address references **Page 3** with some offset. The translation proceeds as:\n\n```text\nVirtual Address → [Page 3 | Offset]\nPage Table Lookup: Page 3 → Frame 5\nPhysical Address → [Frame 5 | Offset]\n```\n\nThe offset remains unchanged; only the page number is replaced by the frame number.\n\n### Example 3: Multiple Processes\n\nSince each process has its own page table, the same virtual page number in two different processes can map to entirely different frames. For instance, **Page 0** in Process A may map to **Frame 3**, while **Page 0** in Process B may map to **Frame 7**. This isolation is a key benefit of paging.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe relationship between virtual and physical addresses can be expressed as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Physical Address} = (\\text{Frame Number} \\times \\text{Page Size}) + \\text{Offset}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Virtual Address} = (\\text{Page Number} \\times \\text{Page Size}) + \\text{Offset}\n$$\n\nThe page table provides the mapping:\n\n$$\nf: \\text{Page Number} \\rightarrow \\text{Frame Number}\n$$\n\nFor the example table:\n\n$$\nf(0) = 3,\\quad f(1) = 7,\\quad f(2) = 1,\\quad f(3) = 5\n$$\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Page Table Mapping | Direct Physical Mapping |\n| ------ | ------------------ | ----------------------- |\n| Address Type | Virtual → Physical | Physical only |\n| Indirection | Yes (via page table) | No |\n| Process Isolation | Yes (per-process tables) | No |\n| Flexibility | High (pages can be anywhere) | Low (fixed locations) |\n| Lookup Overhead | Requires table access | None |\n\n| Page | Frame (Example) |\n| ---- | --------------- |\n| 0 | 3 |\n| 1 | 7 |\n| 2 | 1 |\n| 3 | 5 |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing page number with frame number.** The page number is the virtual index; the frame number is the physical location. They are not interchangeable.\n- **Assuming page tables are shared across processes.** Each process generally has its own page table.\n- **Forgetting the offset.** The page table only translates the page portion of an address; the offset is carried through unchanged.\n- **Mixing up the direction of the mapping.** The page table maps **page → frame**, not frame → page.\n- **Ignoring that the same page number can map to different frames in different processes.**\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the direction of the mapping: **Page → Frame**.\n- Be able to read a page table and answer translation questions quickly, as shown in the example table.\n- Remember that the **offset is unchanged** during translation.\n- Practice converting between virtual and physical addresses using the formula:\n  $$\n  \\text{Physical Address} = (\\text{Frame Number} \\times \\text{Page Size}) + \\text{Offset}\n  $$\n- When given a table like the one above, be prepared to state the frame for any page (e.g., **Page 2 → Frame 1**).\n- Emphasize that page tables enable **process isolation** and **flexible memory placement**.",
