          "id": "segmented-address",
          "title": "Segmented Address",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 3 Concept 50",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Segmented Address: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Segmented Address\n\n### Academic Definition\n\nA **segmented address** is a logical (virtual) address used in a memory-management scheme called **segmentation**, in which the address space is divided into variable-sized logical units called **segments**. Each logical address is expressed as an ordered pair consisting of a **segment number** and an **offset** within that segment. The segment number identifies which segment the reference belongs to, while the offset specifies the displacement of the target location from the beginning (base) of that segment.\n\nSegmentation reflects the programmer's view of memory, where a program is naturally composed of logical divisions such as code, data, stack, and heap. Because segments are of variable length, the hardware must verify that the offset lies within the segment's bounds before translation.\n\nA logical address in segmentation contains:\n\n```text\n┌─────────────────┬────────────┐\n│ Segment Number  │   Offset   │\n└─────────────────┴────────────┘\n```\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe translation of a segmented logical address into a physical address proceeds as follows:\n\n1. The CPU generates a logical address consisting of a **segment number** and an **offset**.\n2. The segment number is used as an index into the **segment table**.\n3. The segment table entry provides the **base** (starting physical address of the segment) and the **limit** (length of the segment).\n4. The hardware checks the offset against the limit:\n   - If `offset < limit`, the reference is **valid**.\n   - If `offset >= limit`, a **segmentation fault / trap** is raised.\n5. If valid, the **physical address** is computed as:\n\n```text\nPhysical Address = Base + Offset\n```\n\nThe segment table contains:\n\n| Segment | Base | Limit |\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\nConsider the following segment table:\n\n| Segment | Base | Limit |\n| ------- | ---- | ----- |\n| 0 | 1000 | 500 |\n| 1 | 4000 | 1000 |\n| 2 | 7000 | 600 |\n\n**Example 1 — Valid access**\n\nFor:\n\n```text\nSegment = 1\nOffset = 250\n```\n\nPhysical address:\n\n```text\nBase + Offset\n= 4000 + 250\n= 4250\n```\n\nSince:\n\n```text\n250 < 1000\n```\n\nthe address is valid.\n\n**Example 2 — Interpreting the table**\n\n- Segment 0 spans physical addresses `1000` to `1000 + 500 = 1500`.\n- Segment 1 spans physical addresses `4000` to `4000 + 1000 = 5000`.\n- Segment 2 spans physical addresses `7000` to `7000 + 600 = 7600`.\n\nAny offset equal to or exceeding the limit for the referenced segment would be rejected by the hardware, even if the resulting physical address happened to fall inside another segment's range.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet the logical address be the pair $(s, d)$, where $s$ is the segment number and $d$ is the offset.\n\nThe segment table entry for segment $s$ is $(base_s, limit_s)$.\n\n**Validity condition:**\n\n$$\nd < limit_s\n$$\n\n**Physical address computation:**\n\n$$\nPA = base_s + d\n$$\n\n**Segment physical extent:**\n\n$$\n\\text{Segment } s \\text{ occupies } [base_s,\\ base_s + limit_s)\n$$\n\nFor the worked example:\n\n$$\nPA = base_1 + d = 4000 + 250 = 4250\n$$\n\nwith validity guaranteed by:\n\n$$\n250 < 1000\n$$\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Segmented Address | Notes |\n| ------ | ----------------- | ----- |\n| Address form | Segment number + offset | Logical address pair |\n| Segment size | Variable | Determined by limit field |\n| Translation table | Segment table | Holds base and limit per segment |\n| Validity check | Offset compared with limit | `offset < limit` required |\n| Physical address | Base + Offset | Simple addition after check |\n| Error condition | Offset ≥ limit | Segmentation fault / trap |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Forgetting the limit check.** Computing `Base + Offset` without first verifying `offset < limit` can produce an address that lies in a different segment, masking an invalid access.\n- **Confusing base with limit.** The **base** is the starting physical address; the **limit** is the length of the segment, not its end address.\n- **Assuming fixed segment sizes.** Unlike paging, segments are variable-sized, so each entry has its own limit.\n- **Treating the offset as a physical address.** The offset is relative to the segment base, not to physical memory.\n- **Ignoring the segment number.** The offset alone is meaningless without the segment number that selects the table entry.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Always show the two-step reasoning: **(1) validity check** (`offset < limit`), then **(2) physical address** (`Base + Offset`).\n- Memorize the address layout: **Segment Number | Offset**.\n- When given a segment table, compute each segment's physical range as `[Base, Base + Limit)` to quickly judge validity.\n- State explicitly that a failed check results in a **segmentation fault / trap**.\n- For the standard example, remember the result: Segment 1, Offset 250 → Physical Address **4250**, valid because **250 < 1000**.\n\n---",
