          "id": "fifo-numerical",
          "title": "FIFO Numerical",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 4 Concept 9",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on FIFO Numerical: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## FIFO Numerical\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**FIFO (First-In-First-Out)** is a page replacement algorithm used in operating systems for virtual memory management. When a page fault occurs and no free frame is available, FIFO replaces the page that has been in memory the **longest** — i.e., the page that was brought into a frame earliest in time. It operates on the principle of a simple queue: the oldest page (the one at the \"front\" of the queue) is evicted first, and the newly referenced page is inserted at the \"rear.\"\n\nFIFO is one of the simplest page replacement policies to implement, requiring only a queue data structure and a pointer to track the oldest frame. It is **not** based on usage frequency or recency of access — only on arrival order.\n\n### Working Principle\n\n1. The operating system maintains a queue of frames in the order pages were loaded.\n2. On a **page hit**, no replacement occurs; the page remains in its frame (its queue position is unchanged in classic FIFO).\n3. On a **page fault**:\n   - If a free frame exists, the page is loaded into it and added to the rear of the queue.\n   - If no free frame exists, the page at the **front** of the queue (oldest) is evicted, and the new page is placed in that frame and moved to the rear of the queue.\n4. The algorithm continues until all references in the reference string are processed.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**Reference string:**\n\n```text\n7, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 4\n```\n\n**Frames = 3.**\n\n| Reference | Frame 1 | Frame 2 | Frame 3 | Result |\n| --------- | ------- | ------- | ------- | ------ |\n| 7 | 7 | - | - | Fault |\n| 0 | 7 | 0 | - | Fault |\n| 1 | 7 | 0 | 1 | Fault |\n| 2 | 2 | 0 | 1 | Fault |\n| 0 | 2 | 0 | 1 | Hit |\n| 3 | 2 | 3 | 1 | Fault |\n| 0 | 2 | 3 | 0 | Fault |\n| 4 | 4 | 3 | 0 | Fault |\n\n**Step-by-step reasoning:**\n\n- **7** → empty frame available → Fault. Queue: `[7]`\n- **0** → empty frame available → Fault. Queue: `[7, 0]`\n- **1** → empty frame available → Fault. Queue: `[7, 0, 1]`\n- **2** → no free frame; evict oldest (7) → Fault. Queue: `[0, 1, 2]`\n- **0** → already in memory → **Hit**. Queue unchanged: `[0, 1, 2]`\n- **3** → no free frame; evict oldest (0) → Fault. Queue: `[1, 2, 3]`\n- **0** → not in memory; evict oldest (1) → Fault. Queue: `[2, 3, 0]`\n- **4** → not in memory; evict oldest (2) → Fault. Queue: `[3, 0, 4]`\n\n**Total page faults:**\n\n```text\n7, 0, 1, 2, 3, 0, 4\n= 7 faults\n```\n\n**Page-fault rate:**\n\n```text\nPage Fault Rate\n= 7 / 8 × 100\n= 87.5%\n```\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\n**Page Fault Rate (PFR):**\n\n$$\n\\text{Page Fault Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Page Faults}}{\\text{Total Number of References}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nFor this example:\n\n$$\n\\text{PFR} = \\frac{7}{8} \\times 100\\% = 87.5\\%\n$$\n\n**Hit Ratio:**\n\n$$\n\\text{Hit Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Hits}}{\\text{Total References}} = \\frac{1}{8} = 12.5\\%\n$$\n\n**Relationship:**\n\n$$\n\\text{Hit Ratio} + \\text{Page Fault Rate} = 100\\%\n$$\n\n**Total references** = 8, **Total faults** = 7, **Total hits** = 1.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | FIFO | (General Comparison Context) |\n| ------ | ---- | ---------------------------- |\n| Replacement basis | Arrival order (oldest evicted) | Other policies use recency/frequency |\n| Data structure | Queue | — |\n| Susceptible to Belady's Anomaly | Yes | Some policies are not |\n| Implementation complexity | Very simple | — |\n| Considers usage pattern | No | — |\n\n> Note: Only FIFO-specific facts from the given notes are asserted here; comparative columns are limited to general properties implied by the FIFO definition.\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Updating queue position on a hit** — In classic FIFO, a hit does **not** change the page's position in the queue. Only insertion order matters.\n2. **Evicting the most recently used page** — FIFO evicts the **oldest**, not the newest. Confusing FIFO with LIFO or LRU leads to wrong frame tables.\n3. **Miscounting faults** — Every reference that is not already present in a frame is a fault, including the first fill of empty frames.\n4. **Forgetting empty-frame faults** — The first references (7, 0, 1) are faults even though no eviction occurs, because frames start empty.\n5. **Incorrect fault-rate denominator** — The denominator is the **total number of references** (8), not the number of frames or faults.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- **Draw the table clearly** with columns for each frame and a \"Result\" column marking Fault/Hit.\n- **Track the queue order** mentally or in the margin to know which page is oldest.\n- **Count faults carefully**: here faults occur at references 7, 0, 1, 2, 3, 0, 4 → **7 faults**.\n- **Remember the formula**: Page Fault Rate = (Faults / Total References) × 100.\n- **Verify**: Hits + Faults = Total References (1 + 7 = 8 ✓).\n- **State the final answer explicitly**: 7 faults and 87.5% page-fault rate.\n- Watch for the **single hit** at reference `0` (5th reference) — it is the only hit in this string.\n\n---",
