          "id": "proportional-allocation",
          "title": "Proportional Allocation",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 4 Concept 17",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Proportional Allocation: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Proportional Allocation\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Proportional Allocation** is a frame allocation policy used in operating systems to distribute the available physical memory frames among competing processes in direct proportion to their sizes. Frames are allocated according to process size.\n\nIn this scheme, larger processes receive a larger share of frames, and smaller processes receive a smaller share, such that the allocation ratio mirrors the ratio of each process's size to the total size of all processes. It is one of the classical allocation strategies (alongside equal allocation) used when multiple processes must share a fixed pool of physical frames.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe core idea is fairness by size: a process that occupies more logical/virtual memory should receive a correspondingly larger number of physical frames. The mechanism proceeds as follows:\n\n1. Determine the total number of available physical frames.\n2. Determine the size of each competing process.\n3. Compute the total process size across all processes.\n4. For each process, compute its share of frames as the fraction of total process size it represents, multiplied by the total available frames.\n5. Assign that many frames to the process.\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nPhysical frames = 100\n\nP1 size = 20 KB\nP2 size = 30 KB\nP3 size = 50 KB\n```\n\nTotal:\n\n```text\n20 + 30 + 50 = 100 KB\n```\n\nFrames:\n\n```text\nP1 = 20 frames\nP2 = 30 frames\nP3 = 50 frames\n```\n\nFormula:\n\n```text\nAllocationᵢ =\n(Process Sizeᵢ / Total Process Size)\n× Total Available Frames\n```\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**Example 1 — Exact proportional split (from the given notes):**\n\n```text\nPhysical frames = 100\nP1 = 20 KB, P2 = 30 KB, P3 = 50 KB\nTotal process size = 100 KB\n\nP1 = (20 / 100) × 100 = 20 frames\nP2 = (30 / 100) × 100 = 30 frames\nP3 = (50 / 100) × 100 = 50 frames\n```\n\nHere the process sizes sum exactly to the total, so the frame allocations sum exactly to the 100 available frames.\n\n**Example 2 — Interpreting the ratio:**\n\nThe allocation preserves the size ratio among processes. Since P1 : P2 : P3 = 20 : 30 : 50, the frames follow the same ratio 20 : 30 : 50. A process that is 50% of the total process size receives 50% of the frames.\n\n**Example 3 — Larger process gets more frames:**\n\nP3 (50 KB) is the largest process and receives the most frames (50), while P1 (20 KB) is the smallest and receives the fewest (20). This demonstrates the defining property of proportional allocation: frame share scales with process size.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe allocation for process *i* is given by:\n\n$$\n\\text{Allocation}_i = \\left( \\frac{\\text{Process Size}_i}{\\text{Total Process Size}} \\right) \\times \\text{Total Available Frames}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{Total Process Size} = \\sum_{i} \\text{Process Size}_i\n$$\n\nEquivalently, the fraction of frames given to process *i* equals its fraction of total process size:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{Allocation}_i}{\\text{Total Available Frames}} = \\frac{\\text{Process Size}_i}{\\text{Total Process Size}}\n$$\n\nThis shows the proportional relationship directly: the frame fraction mirrors the size fraction.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Proportional Allocation | Equal Allocation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Basis of allocation | Process size | Same for every process |\n| Larger processes | Receive more frames | Receive the same as smaller ones |\n| Smaller processes | Receive fewer frames | Receive the same as larger ones |\n| Ratio preserved | Yes — frames follow size ratio | No — all equal |\n| Example (100 frames, sizes 20/30/50 KB) | 20 / 30 / 50 frames | Split equally among processes |\n\n| Process | Size | Proportional frames | Share of total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | 20 KB | 20 | 20% |\n| P2 | 30 KB | 30 | 30% |\n| P3 | 50 KB | 50 | 50% |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing process size with frame count.** The formula uses *process size* in the numerator and *total process size* in the denominator; the result is a number of frames, not a size.\n- **Forgetting to sum all process sizes.** The denominator must be the total process size across all competing processes, not the size of a single process.\n- **Assuming equal allocation.** Proportional allocation does *not* give every process the same number of frames; it deliberately varies the count with size.\n- **Mixing up total available frames with total process size.** In the given example both equal 100, which can mask the distinction — the numerator uses process size, while the multiplier is the total available frames.\n- **Dropping the ratio check.** A quick sanity check is that the frame ratio should match the size ratio (20 : 30 : 50).\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the formula exactly as stated:\n\n```text\nAllocationᵢ =\n(Process Sizeᵢ / Total Process Size)\n× Total Available Frames\n```\n\n- Always compute **Total Process Size** first by summing all process sizes.\n- Show the substitution step clearly, e.g. `(20 / 100) × 100 = 20 frames`.\n- Verify that the individual allocations sum to the total available frames when the sizes sum to the total.\n- Be ready to contrast proportional allocation with equal allocation in a table.\n- Remember the one-line definition: **frames are allocated according to process size.**\n\n---",
