          "id": "file-access-methods",
          "title": "File Access Methods",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 4 Concept 27",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on File Access Methods: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## File Access Methods\n\n**File Access Methods** define the manner in which records within a file are read from or written to secondary storage. The choice of access method determines how the operating system locates a specific record, and it directly influences the efficiency of read/write operations.\n\nImportant file access methods:\n\n1. Sequential access\n2. Direct access\n3. Indexed access\n\n```text\nFile Access\n                   │\n       ┌───────────┼───────────┐\n       ▼           ▼           ▼\n Sequential      Direct      Indexed\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nA **file access method** is the mechanism or technique used by an operating system and application programs to retrieve, insert, modify, or delete records stored in a file. It specifies the order in which records are accessed and the operations permitted on the file. The three classical access methods — **sequential**, **direct**, and **indexed** — differ in how the read/write pointer moves and how a target record is located.\n\n- **Sequential access:** Records are processed in a fixed order, one after another, from the beginning of the file.\n- **Direct access:** Records can be read or written in any order, typically by computing the record's location from its address or key.\n- **Indexed access:** An index (a lookup table) is maintained that maps keys to record locations, allowing fast retrieval without scanning the entire file.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\n### 1. Sequential Access\nThe file is treated as a linear sequence of records. To reach record *n*, all preceding records (1 to *n−1*) must be traversed. The read/write pointer advances only forward (or can be rewound). This mirrors how magnetic tape works.\n\n### 2. Direct Access\nAlso called **relative access**. The file is viewed as a numbered sequence of blocks or records. The system can jump directly to block *k* using a computed address, without reading intervening blocks. This mirrors how magnetic disks and SSDs work.\n\n### 3. Indexed Access\nAn **index** is built containing entries of the form *(key → block address)*. To find a record, the system searches the index for the key, obtains the block address, and then performs a direct access to that block. This combines the speed of direct access with key-based lookup.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n| Method | Real-World Analogy | Typical Use Case |\n|--------|--------------------|------------------|\n| Sequential | Reading a cassette tape from start to end | Log files, batch processing, compiler input |\n| Direct | Jumping to track 7 on a CD | Database records, random-access files |\n| Indexed | Using a book's index to find a page | Large databases, ISAM files, library catalogs |\n\n**Sequential example:** A payroll program reads employee records one by one to print payslips.\n\n**Direct example:** A reservation system directly accesses seat record number 42 to check availability.\n\n**Indexed example:** A student database uses a roll-number index to locate a student's record instantly.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet a file contain **N** records, and let the target be record number **k**.\n\n**Sequential access — average records scanned:**\n$$E_{\\text{seq}} = \\frac{N+1}{2}$$\n\n**Direct access — records scanned:**\n$$E_{\\text{direct}} = 1$$\n\n**Indexed access — records scanned (index of size m):**\n$$E_{\\text{indexed}} = \\log_2 m + 1 \\quad \\text{(binary search on index)}$$\n\n**Time complexity comparison:**\n\n| Method | Best Case | Average Case | Worst Case |\n|--------|-----------|--------------|------------|\n| Sequential | O(1) | O(N) | O(N) |\n| Direct | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) |\n| Indexed | O(1) | O(log m) | O(log m) |\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Feature | Sequential Access | Direct Access | Indexed Access |\n|---------|-------------------|---------------|----------------|\n| Access order | Fixed, linear | Arbitrary | Key-based |\n| Pointer movement | Forward only | Any position | Via index |\n| Storage medium | Magnetic tape | Disk / SSD | Disk with index |\n| Speed for random read | Slow | Fast | Fast |\n| Speed for sequential read | Fast | Fast | Moderate |\n| Extra storage overhead | None | None | Index table required |\n| Complexity | Low | Medium | High |\n| Suitability | Batch processing | Random access files | Large keyed databases |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Confusing direct access with indexed access.** Direct access uses a computed address; indexed access uses a key-to-address lookup table.\n2. **Assuming sequential access cannot be rewound.** Many systems allow rewinding, but forward traversal remains the dominant mode.\n3. **Believing indexed access eliminates the need for direct access.** Indexed access *relies on* direct access to fetch the located block.\n4. **Ignoring index maintenance cost.** Every insert/delete requires updating the index, adding overhead.\n5. **Mixing up physical and logical access.** Access methods are logical; they are implemented on top of physical storage.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Define each method in one line** — examiners award marks for crisp definitions.\n- **Draw the tree diagram** (Sequential / Direct / Indexed) for quick recall.\n- **Use the comparison table** to answer \"differentiate between\" questions.\n- **Mention the storage medium** (tape vs disk) as it strengthens the answer.\n- **State the time complexity** where relevant — it demonstrates depth.\n- **Give one real-world example per method** to secure application marks.\n- **Remember the relationship:** Indexed access = Index + Direct access.\n- **Avoid over-explaining**; keep answers structured with headings and bullet points.",
