          "id": "indexed-allocation",
          "title": "Indexed Allocation",
          "subtitle": "CA456 Unit 4 Concept 45",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Indexed Allocation: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Indexed Allocation\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Indexed Allocation** is a file allocation method in which each file has its own **index block** — a dedicated block that contains an array of pointers to the file's data blocks. Instead of storing the addresses of a file's blocks inside a linked list scattered across the disk (as in linked allocation) or requiring a contiguous run of blocks (as in contiguous allocation), indexed allocation centralizes all block pointers into a single structure: the index block.\n\nFormally, for a file `F` consisting of `n` data blocks, the index block holds `n` entries, where the `i`-th entry points to the physical disk block containing the `i`-th logical block of the file. The index block itself occupies one disk block (or more, in extended schemes), and the file's metadata (in the inode or file control block) stores the address of the index block.\n\nThis scheme decouples the logical ordering of a file from its physical placement on disk, providing both **direct access** and **non-contiguous storage**.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe operation of indexed allocation proceeds as follows:\n\n1. **File creation:** When a file is created, the file system allocates one block to serve as the **index block**. The address of this index block is recorded in the file's inode/FCB.\n2. **Block allocation:** As data is written, free data blocks are allocated anywhere on disk. Their addresses are stored sequentially in the index block's pointer array.\n3. **Logical-to-physical mapping:** To access logical block `i` of the file, the system:\n   - Reads the index block (if not already cached).\n   - Retrieves the pointer at entry `i`.\n   - Uses that pointer to access the physical data block directly.\n4. **Direct access:** Because entry `i` can be indexed in constant time, any block of the file can be reached without traversing preceding blocks — enabling random/direct access.\n\nThe structure can be visualized as:\n\n```text\nIndex Block\n          ┌──────────────┐\n          │ 9            │\n          │ 16           │\n          │ 1            │\n          │ 10           │\n          └──────┬───────┘\n                 │\n       ┌─────────┼─────────┐\n       ▼         ▼         ▼\n     Block 9   Block 16   Block 1\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                           Block 10\n```\n\nHere, the index block contains four pointers (9, 16, 1, 10). Logical block 0 → physical block 9, logical block 1 → physical block 16, logical block 2 → physical block 1, and logical block 3 → physical block 10. Note that the physical blocks are scattered and need not be contiguous.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**Example 1 — Small file:** A file of 4 blocks uses one index block with 4 valid entries. Accessing the 3rd block requires reading the index block, then following entry 2 (zero-indexed) to the physical block.\n\n**Example 2 — Large file:** If a disk block is 4 KB and a pointer is 4 bytes, one index block holds `4096 / 4 = 1024` pointers. A file larger than 1024 blocks requires either **linked index blocks**, a **multilevel index**, or a **combined scheme** (as in UNIX inodes with direct, single-, double-, and triple-indirect pointers).\n\n**Example 3 — Sparse access:** A program seeks directly to logical block 500. With indexed allocation, the system reads the index block and jumps straight to entry 500 — no traversal of blocks 0–499 is needed. This is the key advantage over linked allocation.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet:\n\n- `B` = block size in bytes\n- `P` = pointer size in bytes\n- `N` = number of pointers per index block\n\nThen:\n\n$$\nN = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{B}{P} \\right\\rfloor\n$$\n\nThe maximum file size supported by a **single-level** index block is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Max File Size} = N \\times B = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{B}{P} \\right\\rfloor \\times B\n$$\n\nFor a **two-level** index (index of index blocks):\n\n$$\n\\text{Max File Size} = N^2 \\times B\n$$\n\nFor a **three-level** index:\n\n$$\n\\text{Max File Size} = N^3 \\times B\n$$\n\n**Overhead ratio** (fraction of space consumed by index pointers for a fully utilized file):\n\n$$\n\\text{Overhead} = \\frac{P}{B}\n$$\n\nFor example, with `B = 4096` and `P = 4`, overhead is `4/4096 ≈ 0.098%` per data block, plus one index block per file.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Criterion | Contiguous Allocation | Linked Allocation | **Indexed Allocation** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Direct access | Yes | No | **Yes** |\n| Contiguity required | Yes | No | **No** |\n| External fragmentation | Yes | No | **No** |\n| Extra space overhead | None | Pointer per block | **Index block per file** |\n| Reliability (single pointer loss) | N/A | Breaks chain | **Index block loss breaks file** |\n| Suitability for large files | Poor (fragmentation) | Good | **Good (with multilevel)** |\n| Suitability for small files | Good | Good | Index block may be wasteful |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing index block with data block:** The index block stores *pointers*, not file data. It is metadata.\n- **Assuming no overhead:** Indexed allocation consumes at least one full block per file for the index, which is wasteful for very small files.\n- **Ignoring multilevel indexing:** A single index block cannot address arbitrarily large files; students often forget the need for indirect/multilevel schemes.\n- **Mixing up with linked allocation:** In linked allocation, pointers are stored *within* data blocks; in indexed allocation, pointers are *separated* into an index block.\n- **Assuming contiguity:** Physical blocks in indexed allocation are scattered; only the logical order is preserved via the index.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Always state the **two key advantages**: direct access and non-contiguous allocation.\n- Mention the **disadvantage**: additional index-block space overhead.\n- If asked to compare, use the comparative table above — it covers the three classical allocation methods.\n- For numerical problems, remember `N = B / P` and the multilevel formula `N^k × B`.\n- Draw the index-block diagram — it clearly demonstrates the pointer-to-data-block relationship and earns presentation marks.\n- Link this concept to **inode-based file systems** (UNIX/Linux) where direct, single-, double-, and triple-indirect pointers implement multilevel indexed allocation.",
